    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The McKim Limestone unit is a well-exposed, 3m- thick stratum that stretches over much of Raplee anticline and across the antiformal hinge of Comb monocline as nearly continuous, kilometer-scale outcrops in some areas. Two sets of systematic, carbonate-filled, echelon veins and complementary echelon pressure solution seams are common and apparently present only within this unit. Each set maintains sub-parallel orientations across those parts of the folds with modest limb dips. Left-stepping and right-stepping echelon vein arrays have mean trends of 097°and 140°, respectively. These two sets of approximately planar shear zones often cross-cut in a conjugate geometry. They also are observed to abut one another or appear as only one set. The initial formation of thin echelon veins suggests a brittle elastic deformation. However, some veins have greater apertures to trace length ratios, and some have sigmoidal shapes, suggesting ductile inelastic deformation.\r\n\r\nCurrent conceptual models of brittle-ductile shear zones of this type infer principal strain and stress orientations based on geometries and displacement indicators, such that the regional greatest compressive stress bisects the acute angle between a pair of conjugate shear zones at the time of formation. Previous studies have suggested that the shear strain across a localized zone produces echelon extension fractures, whose initial orientation within the zone is defined by the local principal strain direction.\r\n\r\nOther limestone and grainstone strata in this field area do not exhibit conjugate shear zones with echelon vein arrays. A suggestion is that the specific compositional structure of the McKim Limestone favored vein formation over jointing under the same stress state. These observations open the door to investigate the constitutive properties of the different lithologies and to compare models of brittle-ductile shear zones based on a complete mechanics to those based on kinematic relationships alone. The final objective of such an investigation would be to relate the causative stress field to the regional tectonic history.",
